INT. FUNERAL HOME - REPOSITORY ROOM - AFTERNOON

Folding chairs. A coffee urn. Framed photos on an easel. The last guests are drifting toward the door.

MARGUERITE OKAFOR-LINDQVIST (60s, black dress) stands by the coffin, one hand resting on its edge. Her son THEO (30s, loosened tie) collects paper cups. Her daughter ASHA (20s, red-eyed) folds programs into her purse. Her brother DESMOND (50s, rumpled suit) stares at the carpet. Her aunt GERTRUDE (80s, cane, hat with a veil) sits very upright and says nothing. And on the far side of the room, a younger woman waits near the guest book.

Marguerite sets down her handkerchief, crosses to the coffee urn, pours two cups, hands one to Desmond, then sits beside Gertrude. Theo drops a cup into the trash. Asha zips her purse.

The younger woman steps forward. NINA ABERNATHY (30s, gray coat, hair tied back) pauses at the edge of the circle.

NINA
I'm so sorry. I didn't know whether I should come.

Everyone looks at her. Marguerite sets down her cup.

MARGUERITE
Who are you?

NINA
Nina. I worked with your husband.

DESMOND
(low)
I don't remember a Nina.

NINA
I was the night shift.

Gertrude lifts her veil an inch, studies Nina, and lowers it again.

MARGUERITE
Thank you for coming.

NINA
He talked about you often.

Marguerite nods. She places a hand on Nina's arm, gently, and walks her to the door.
